<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · c. 1900 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Day-O (The Banana Boat Song)</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A work song of Jamaican dock workers who loaded bananas onto ships all night and wanted to go home when day came.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional; Belafonte's version by Lord Burgess and William Attaway (1955)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jamaican dock workers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (Jamaican)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jamaica</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>around the beginning of the 20th century; first recorded 1952</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Jamaican work song; the words Lord Burgess and William Attaway wrote for Harry Belafonte in 1955 are in copyright)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Day-O (The Banana Boat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>)&quot; is a traditional Jamaican folk song with mento influences, though it is usually classed as calypso. It probably arose around the beginning of the 20th century, when the banana trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> was growing. Dock workers loaded bananas onto the ocean-going cargo ships called banana boats at night, to avoid the heat of the day. It is a call-and-response work song in their voice: when daylight came their shift was over, and they waited for their boss to tally the bananas so that they could go home.</p><p>The Trinidadian singer Edric Connor and his band the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbeans</a> made the first recording, &quot;Day Dah Light&quot;, on the 1952 album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, and Louise Bennett recorded it in 1954. In 1955 the American songwriters Lord Burgess and William Attaway wrote a version of the words for The Colgate Comedy Hour, where Harry Belafonte sang it. Belafonte, who based his version on Connor's and Bennett's recordings, recorded it for RCA Victor; it came out in 1956 on his album Calypso and as a single, reached number five on the Billboard charts in 1957 and became his signature song.</p><p>In the same year the folk singer Bob Gibson, who had heard the song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, taught his version to the folk band the Tarriers (Erik Darling, Bob Carey and Alan Arkin). Their recording joined it to the chorus of another Jamaican call-and-response song, &quot;Hill and Gully Rider&quot;, and did better than Belafonte's on the pop charts; the Fontane Sisters, Sarah Vaughan and Steve Lawrence in 1956 and Shirley Bassey in 1957 all had hits with that version. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, Michiko Hamamura became famous as the &quot;Banana Boat Girl&quot; in 1957.</p><p>In a 2011 interview with Gwen Ifill on PBS NewsHour, Belafonte described it as a song about struggle, about Black people in a colonised life doing the most grueling work, and said he had made it into an anthem the world loved. Its call and response has lived on: Freddie Mercury of Queen improvised his famous exchange with the crowd at Live Aid in 1985 from its opening, and its chorus is used in many Major League Baseball stadiums. It has been parodied, sampled and used in advertising many times, and Belafonte's 1956 recording is heard in the 1988 film Beetlejuice.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song speaks for dock workers at the end of a night shift loading bananas: day has come, their work is done, and they want it counted so that they can go home. Its call and response carries the weariness of hard labour and the longing for rest.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="H5dpBWlRANE" data-title="Day-O (The Banana Boat Song): Harry Belafonte, live (his 1956 version)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Harry Belafonte, live (his 1956 version)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Harry Belafonte Television and Video Archive. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H5dpBWlRANE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Banana Boat (Day-O) (Harry Belafonte)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new version of the words by Lord Burgess and William Attaway</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Banana Boat Song (the Tarriers)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">version joined with the chorus of &quot;Hill and Gully Rider&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Star O (Harry Belafonte)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">shorter reprise with different words, on Calypso</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Banana Boat (Day-O) (Stan Freberg and Billy May)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Het Bananenlied (André van Duin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Theo (Der Bananenbrot-Song) (Rolf Zuckowski)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1983</span><span class="sg-chain-h">children's song on the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dejo majstore (the Kuguars)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Serbian cover with new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">6 Foot 7 Foot (Lil Wayne)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled; title taken from it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Don't Wanna Go Home (Jason Derulo)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Banana (Conkarah)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adaptation of Belafonte's version</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Day Dah Light (Edric Connor and the Caribbeans)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1952</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first recording; a source of Belafonte's version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by Louise Bennett</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1954</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a source of Belafonte's version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hill and Gully Rider</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorus joined to the song in the Tarriers' version</span></li></ul></section>
